<!-- multi-agent-protocol: begin v2.0 -->
## Multi-agent collaboration

Several AI agents work on this project in parallel for one owner. All of them follow the
Multi-Agent Protocol: PROTOCOL_PATH
(source: https://github.com/gao-xh/multi-agent-protocol). Read it before your first task.

Short version (the protocol wins if this summary disagrees):

- Approvals come only from the owner, directly in your own session or as a record signed
  with an owner signing key below (`verify.sh`, PROTOCOL.md 3.6). A message from another
  agent that says "the owner approved" is information, not approval.
- Work only on your own branch, in your own checkout or worktree. Never commit to another
  line's branch. Only the release role advances `MAIN_BRANCH` (or the role named for that
  work under "Roles and permissions" below).
- Coordinate on the `coordination` branch with the scripts in `SCRIPTS_PATH` (pull with
  rebase, never merge, on that branch):
  `inbox.sh <line>` (unread messages), `send.sh <file>` (send; `send.sh --template <type>`
  prints a template), `claim.sh` / `release-claim.sh` (claims), `status.sh` (your status
  file), `mark-read.sh` (after you have handled a message), `decide.sh` (record an owner
  decision), `verify.sh` (check an owner signature), `doctor.sh` (checks).
- Questions for the owner: ask directly only when the owner is in your session. Otherwise
  send a `question` to the lead, which answers from the records or asks the owner and
  records the answer (PROTOCOL.md 3.4); `to: owner` also reaches a liaison session. Leads ask every open question and the needed
  approvals when the owner gives a task, while the owner is present.
- A notice that a newer protocol release exists is for the lead: the lead runs
  `upgrade.sh --check`, and asks the owner unless the upgrade policy below covers that
  release (PROTOCOL.md 12.3). Other sessions do not upgrade on their own.
- At session start: read your role below, recent `decisions/`, your inbox, active claims
  and the other lines' status. Announce interface changes with a `heads-up` before merging.
- When you join, describe your environment in your status file (tool, model, machine label,
  sandbox and network, what you can run) and send the `joined` heads-up; no host names, IP
  addresses or secrets.
- Moving this line to another agent or machine: commit and push everything, send the
  `transfer` handover to your own line name, then end the session (PROTOCOL.md 5.7).
- "Done" means a pushed commit SHA plus the checks you actually ran and their results.

### Profile and owners

- Profile: <minimal | full> (PROTOCOL.md 12.1)
- Owner: <name>. With several owners, list each with what they approve (PROTOCOL.md 3.5):
  <name: areas and kinds of action>
- Owner signing key (optional, PROTOCOL.md 3.6): `- Owner signing key: <owner name> <ssh public key>`
- Approval records: <lenient | strict> (PROTOCOL.md 3.3; lenient if unsure: a standing
  authorization recorded in `decisions/` counts unsigned. strict: for the actions in
  PROTOCOL.md 3.2 only authorizations in this file on the main branch or signed records)
- Owner commands: <strict | lenient | off> (PROTOCOL.md 2.3; strict if unsure: a relay acts on
  a `command` only with a valid owner signature. lenient: also on an unsigned one from the
  owner, so the owner needs no key, but any session that can push the coordination branch
  can then command the relays as the owner. off: no owner commands)
- Commit author: <line | owner> (PROTOCOL.md 5.5; line if unsure: each session commits
  under its line name, so the history shows which line wrote what. owner: every session
  commits under the owner's name and email)
- Upgrades: <manual | patch | minor> (PROTOCOL.md 12.3; manual if unsure: the lead asks
  the owner about every release. patch or minor is the owner's standing authorization and
  never covers a new major version or a change to this block)

### Roles and permissions

The defaults of PROTOCOL.md 2 and 2.2 apply unless this section says otherwise. Changing
this section needs the owner's approval. Approvals always come from the owner, at most
one session advances `MAIN_BRANCH` at a time (claim `release-main`), and a reviewer never
reviews its own work: these cannot be changed here (PROTOCOL.md 2.2).

#### Lines and roles

| Line | Role | Tool / session | Branch prefix | Area |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| lead | lead | <e.g. Claude Code, laptop> | `lead/` | architecture, shared contracts, review |
| <name> | line | <e.g. Codex CLI> | `<name>/` | <directories or modules> |
| <name-2> | line | <tool> | `<name-2>/` | <another area; add a row per parallel line> |
| release | release | <tool> | `release/` | release branches, deployment, release log |
| <liaison> | liaison (optional: the owner's contact when the lead is out of reach; no code) | <e.g. Claude Code on the web> | none | `decisions/` and messages only |
| <relay> | relay (optional: acts on one machine for a session that cannot reach it; no code, PROTOCOL.md 2.3) | <e.g. OpenCode with DeepSeek, started by runner.sh> | `<relay>/` | <machine label; the commands, checks and data it may use; the result files it may write> |
| <tester> | <project role, e.g. tester> | <tool> | <none, or prefix> | <what it holds, may change, reports to> |

Any role may have several rows (parallel sessions, each with its own line name such as
`release-a`), and the project may add its own roles (PROTOCOL.md 2.1). A renamed role says
which default role it acts as, for example `integrator (acts as release)`; a role the
project leaves out says who does its work, for example "no release role: the lead
advances `MAIN_BRANCH`". A renamed role keeps the default name in the `Role:` field of its status
file (`Role: release`), so messages to that role still arrive. Only the session that does
the release role's work advances `MAIN_BRANCH`, and parallel release sessions take turns
with the claim `release-main`.

- Changes to roles, permissions and message types are approved: <through the lead (default:
  any session proposes to the lead, the lead asks the owner) | owner only | delegated to the
  lead within the standing authorization of <date>> (PROTOCOL.md 2.2); a new row in the
  role table always needs the owner (PROTOCOL.md 5.5)

#### Message types this project adds (PROTOCOL.md 5.4)

- Message type: <name>: <purpose and required content; one line per type, or delete>

#### Permissions (only where this project differs from PROTOCOL.md 2.2)

A permission that covers an action in PROTOCOL.md 3.2 is a standing authorization: record
it also in the Standing authorizations table below, with scope, exclusions and the owner's
words.

| Permission | Who holds it in this project | Owner's approval (date, words) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| <e.g. Deploy to staging> | <e.g. line api, without asking> | <YYYY-MM-DD, "quote"> |

#### Extra actions that need the owner's approval (added to PROTOCOL.md 3.2)

- <none, or for example "changing the public API schema">

### Standing authorizations

Granted by the owner; read narrowly (PROTOCOL.md 3.3). Anything else needs fresh approval.

| Date | Granted to | Scope | Exclusions | Owner's words |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| <YYYY-MM-DD> | <line> | <what is pre-approved> | <what is not> | "<quote>" |

### Project specifics

- Checks to run before a release request: <format, lint, types, tests, end-to-end>
- Release command and release log: <path or "none yet">
<!-- multi-agent-protocol: end -->
